  <p>
    As a final note about correlation, this tool only measures how
    something fits. <xref ref="figure-correlation8"/> is one last
    correlation figure.  
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-correlation8">
    <caption><m>r = 0.14</m></caption>
    <image xml:id="image-correlation8" width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            xlabel={Variable 1},
            ylabel={Variable 2},
            ]
            \addplot+ [only marks] table [
              x=var1,
              y=var2,
              col sep=comma,
              row sep=crcr
            ] {
              var1,var2\\
              2,2\\
              3,4\\
              5,17\\
              8,22\\
              11,35\\
              15,37\\
              16,37\\
              17,44\\
              21,49\\
              25,50\\
              32,42\\
              33,41\\
              34,31\\
              37,22\\
              41,20\\
              42,12\\
              49,11\\
            };
            \draw (0,0) -- (560,560);
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    This scatter plot <xref ref="figure-correlation8"/> has low
    positive correlation, r=0.14. There is a very slight positive
    correlation. However, the correlation coefficiententirely misses
    the fact that there is another kind of relationship here. A better
    model for this data would be a function that rises and then falls.
    Such a function is not a linear model, so the correlation number
    doesn't see it at all. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In statistics, linear regression is almost always the first thing
    to try. But some data has relationships which are not linear. It's
    very valuable to remember that linear models are only one of many
    kind of models of relationship between data. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let me define the second measure of a linear model, which is
    very closely connected to correlation.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The square of the correlation is simply called the <m>R^2</m>
        measure. In some contexts, this is called the
        <term>coefficient of determination</term>. However, simply
        referring to it as <m>R^2</m> is so common that I'll stick
        with that convention. The fact that we've changed from a
        lower case <sq>r</sq> to an upper case <sq>R</sq> is annoying,
        but again this is the conventional notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    By squaring the correlation, a couple things happen. First, the
    value of <m>R^2</m> is always positive, so it ignores the
    difference between positive and negative slopes. Close to 0 means
    that the data probably doesn't fit a linear model well, and close
    to 1 means a good fit. In this way, <m>R^2</m> is a slightly more
    elegant measure of linearity, and it gets used more often because
    of this. Second, by squaring, the numbers get slightly smaller.
    (If you square a number less than 1, it gets closer to zero).
    Therefore, <m>R^2</m> is a bit of a finer measure: <m>R^2 =
    0.9</m> is a stronger indication of a good linear fit than
    a correlation of the same value. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In the previous section, I also talked about multiple regression:
    making linear models with multiple explanatory variables. There
    are extension of <m>R^2</m> to multiple regression as well. The
    one which we will use in the classroom activities is called
    <term>adjusted <m>R^2</m></term>. You can assume that adjusted
    <m>R^2</m> means that we must be dealing with several explanatory
    variables. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Now let me get back to residuals, since these can also be used to
    evaluate the quality of a linear regression. Residuals were
    defined in the last section: they are the difference, for each
    data point, bewteen the actual data, <m>y</m>, and the prediction
    of the linear regression, <m>\hat{y}</m>. There is a residual for
    each data point: n in total, where n is the number of cases. 
  </p>
  <p>
    From the previous section, recall <xref
    ref="figure-linear-model"/>. It has a linear model with equation
    <m>\hat{y} = 41 + 0.59x</m>. The residuals are the difference
    between the y coordinate of the data point and the line. However,
    residuals are a bit tricky to see in the original scatterplot. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead, I can just plot the residuals themselves. <xref
    ref="figure-residuals"/> shows the same data set, but the <m>y</m>
    axis is now the residual compared to the linear regression. The
    <m>y</m> value of each data point is simpliy how far it was, in
    the original diagram, away from the line. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A scatterplot where the <m>y</m> axis is the residual of a
        linear regression, instead of the original <m>y</m> value of
        the data point, is called a <term>residual plot</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
