          coordinates { (74,0) (99,0) };
        \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    As a visualization tool, the residual plot is extremely useful to
    seeing how good the regression is, since all it shows is the error
    for each data point. The shape and patterns of the residual plot
    can give information.
  </p>
  <p>
    In general, if there is no pattern to the residual plot (and the
    residuals are not too large), that is good evidence that the
    original regression was pretty good. For the data shown in <xref
    ref="figure-residuals"/>, from the previous section, there isn't
    much of a pattern to the residual plot, so the linear regression
    is probably a decent model of the relationship between the
    variables. 
  </p>
  <p>
    <xref ref="figure-linear-model-2"/> is another scatter plot with a
    linear regression. This looks like a pretty good regression. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-linear-model-2">
    <caption>Another Linear Model</caption>
    <image xml:id="image-linear-model-2" width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            xlabel={x},
